---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { CADENCES, COUNTS, DELIVERY_STATES, SYNC, Spell, spell } from '../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/background-sync/');

const healthyStates = DELIVERY_STATES.filter((state) => state.healthy).length;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    A cadence is a <em>request</em>, not a schedule. iOS decides when Hozz runs. Hozz reports the
    actual state instead of claiming an on-time delivery.
  </p>

  <h2>The three constraints</h2>

  <h3>Health cannot be read while the phone is locked</h3>

  <p>
    Health may be unreadable while locked. Hozz then shows:
  </p>

  <blockquote>Health data is locked. Unlock this iPhone and Hozz will continue.</blockquote>

  <p>
    Its cursor does not advance, so the next pass retries the same records.
  </p>

  <h3>Most Health types are capped at hourly</h3>

  <p>
    Hozz requests <code>.immediate</code>; HealthKit lowers most types to hourly. “When new data
    arrives” therefore does not mean within seconds.
  </p>

  <h3>Force-quitting the app stops background launches</h3>

  <p>
    Swiping Hozz out of the app switcher stops background launches until you reopen it.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Low Power Mode and Background App Refresh">
    <p>
      Low Power Mode and disabled <strong>Background App Refresh</strong> suspend background work.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What the cadences actually mean</h2>

  <p>
    A cadence sets the shortest gap between deliveries. iOS sets no deadline.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Sync cadences and the minimum interval each enforces"
    columns={['Cadence', 'What it means', 'Floor']}
  >
    {
      CADENCES.map((cadence) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{cadence.name}</th>
          <td>
            {cadence.id === 'manual'
              ? 'Only when you tap Sync now, or a Shortcut runs.'
              : 'Deliver when iOS next wakes Hozz and this much time has passed.'}
          </td>
          <td>{cadence.floor}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    Hozz requests another refresh {SYNC.refreshMinutes} minutes after each run. That is an earliest
    acceptable time, not an iOS promise.
  </p>

  <h2>How long a first backfill takes</h2>

  <p>
    A pass reads <strong
      >at most {SYNC.batchRecordLimit.toLocaleString('en-US')} records, or {
        SYNC.batchMegabyteLimit
      } MB</strong
    >, whichever comes first. Every type gets a share; iOS decides how many passes run.
  </p>

  <p>
    Large archives take <strong>days or weeks</strong>. Records arrive throughout.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why there is no percentage and no estimate">
    <p>
      HealthKit reveals no total before reading a type. Hozz shows types started, types finished and
      records sent.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>How to make it run right now</h2>

  <p>
    Open Hozz and tap <strong>Sync now</strong>. Hozz also provides two Shortcuts:{' '}
    <strong>Sync Health Data</strong> and <strong>Check Health Sync Status</strong> — so a personal
    automation can trigger one.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why “Export now” sometimes waits">
    <p>
      One process writes the spool at a time. If opening Hozz started an automatic sync, manual
      export waits and names that activity.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Reading the dashboard</h2>

  <p>
    The dashboard shows one of {spell(COUNTS.deliveryStates)} states per destination. {
      Spell(healthyStates)
    } of them are healthy; only {spell(COUNTS.deliveryStates - healthyStates)} mean something is wrong.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Delivery states and what each means"
    columns={['State', 'Healthy', 'Meaning']}
    mono
  >
    {
      DELIVERY_STATES.map((state) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{state.id}</th>
          <td>{state.healthy ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</td>
          <td>{state.meaning}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    <code>waitingForSystem</code> and <code>waitingForUnlock</code> resolve themselves.{' '}
    <code>retrying</code> keeps trying; <code>needsAttention</code> stops for you.
  </p>

  <h2>What Hozz guarantees regardless</h2>

  <p>
    Each Health type advances only after durable staging. Each destination advances only after
    acceptance, so failures cannot skip data or block another destination.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>An interruption can repeat work, but it cannot skip records.</strong> Content-derived{' '}
    <code>Idempotency-Key</code> values make repeats identifiable.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a> covers what to do when a state is genuinely stuck.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
